{"task_id": "HumanEval/16", "solution": "```python\ndef count_distinct_characters(string: str) -> int:\n    \"\"\" Given a string, find out how many distinct characters (regardless of case) does it consist of\n    >>> count_distinct_characters('xyzXYZ')\n    3\n    >>> count_distinct_characters('Jerry')\n    4\n    \"\"\"\n    # Convert the string to lowercase to treat 'a' and 'A' as the same character\n    lowercase_string = string.lower()\n\n    # Use a set to store unique characters. Sets automatically handle duplicates.\n    distinct_chars = set()\n\n    # Iterate through the lowercase string and add each character to the set\n    for char in lowercase_string:\n        distinct_chars.add(char)\n\n    # The size of the set will be the number of distinct characters\n    return len(distinct_chars)\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/17", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\n\ndef parse_music(music_string: str) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\" Input to this function is a string representing musical notes in a special ASCII format.\n    Your task is to parse this string and return list of integers corresponding to how many beats does each\n    not last.\n\n    Here is a legend:\n    'o' - whole note, lasts four beats\n    'o|' - half note, lasts two beats\n    '.|' - quater note, lasts one beat\n\n    >>> parse_music('o o| .| o| o| .| .| .| .| o o')\n    [4, 2, 1, 2, 2, 1, 1, 1, 1, 4, 4]\n    \"\"\"\n    # Define a mapping from note symbols to their corresponding beat values.\n    note_beats_map = {\n        'o': 4,   # whole note lasts four beats\n        'o|': 2,  # half note lasts two beats\n        '.|': 1   # quarter note lasts one beat\n    }\n\n    # Split the input string into individual note symbols.\n    # Using .split() without arguments handles multiple spaces and leading/trailing\n    # spaces gracefully, returning an empty list for an empty or purely whitespace string.\n    note_symbols = music_string.split()\n\n    # Iterate through the list of note symbols and map each symbol to its beat value.\n    # A list comprehension provides a concise way to do this.\n    beats_list = [note_beats_map[symbol] for symbol in note_symbols]\n\n    return beats_list\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/18", "solution": "```python\ndef how_many_times(string: str, substring: str) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Counts overlapping occurrences of a substring in a string.\n\n    This function iterates through the main string and checks if the substring\n    starts at each possible position, including positions that would cause\n    overlaps with previously found occurrences.\n\n    Args:\n        string: The main string to search within.\n        substring: The substring to count occurrences of.\n\n    Returns:\n        The total number of overlapping occurrences of the substring in the string.\n\n    Examples:\n        >>> how_many_times('', 'a')\n        0\n        >>> how_many_times('aaa', 'a')\n        3\n        >>> how_many_times('aaaa', 'aa')\n        3\n        >>> how_many_times('banana', 'ana')\n        2\n        >>> how_many_times('ababa', 'aba')\n        2\n        >>> how_many_times('hello', 'world')\n        0\n        >>> how_many_times('test', '') # Empty substring handled as 0 occurrences\n        0\n    \"\"\"\n    count = 0\n\n    # Handle the edge case where the substring is empty.\n    # Python's built-in str.count() raises a ValueError for an empty substring.\n    # For this problem, returning 0 is a reasonable and safe interpretation,\n    # avoiding the complexity of defining what \"overlapping occurrences\" means\n    # for an empty string or the \"len(string) + 1\" convention for non-overlapping counts.\n    if not substring:\n        return 0\n\n    # Iterate through the main string to find potential starting positions of the substring.\n    # The loop should run from index 0 up to the point where the substring\n    # can still completely fit within the remaining part of the string.\n    # For example, if string length is L and substring length is S,\n    # the last possible starting index for the substring is L - S.\n    # So, the range for 'i' should be from 0 to L - S (inclusive).\n    # range(N) goes from 0 to N-1, so we need range(L - S + 1).\n    for i in range(len(string) - len(substring) + 1):\n        # Check if the slice of the string from 'i' to 'i + len(substring)'\n        # matches the substring.\n        if string[i : i + len(substring)] == substring:\n            count += 1\n\n    return count\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/19", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\n\ndef sort_numbers(numbers: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\" Input is a space-delimited string of numberals from 'zero' to 'nine'.\n    Valid choices are 'zero', 'one', 'two', 'three', 'four', 'five', 'six', 'seven', 'eight' and 'nine'.\n    Return the string with numbers sorted from smallest to largest\n    >>> sort_numbers('three one five')\n    'one three five'\n    >>> sort_numbers('nine two zero eight')\n    'zero two eight nine'\n    >>> sort_numbers('seven')\n    'seven'\n    >>> sort_numbers('')\n    ''\n    >>> sort_numbers('one one one')\n    'one one one'\n    \"\"\"\n    if not numbers:\n        return \"\"\n\n    # Mapping from number word to its integer value\n    word_to_int = {\n        'zero': 0, 'one': 1, 'two': 2, 'three': 3, 'four': 4,\n        'five': 5, 'six': 6, 'seven': 7, 'eight': 8, 'nine': 9\n    }\n\n    # Mapping from integer value to its number word\n    int_to_word = {\n        0: 'zero', 1: 'one', 2: 'two', 3: 'three', 4: 'four',\n        5: 'five', 6: 'six', 7: 'seven', 8: 'eight', 9: 'nine'\n    }\n\n    # 1. Split the input string into individual number words\n    number_words = numbers.split()\n\n    # 2. Convert each number word to its integer representation\n    integer_numbers = [word_to_int[word] for word in number_words]\n\n    # 3. Sort the list of integers\n    sorted_integers = sorted(integer_numbers)\n\n    # 4. Convert the sorted integers back to their number word representation\n    sorted_number_words = [int_to_word[num] for num in sorted_integers]\n\n    # 5. Join the sorted number words back into a space-delimited string\n    return ' '.join(sorted_number_words)\n```"}
