{"task_id": "HumanEval/35", "solution": "```python\ndef max_element(l: list):\n    \"\"\"Return maximum element in the list.\n    >>> max_element([1, 2, 3])\n    3\n    >>> max_element([5, 3, -5, 2, -3, 3, 9, 0, 123, 1, -10])\n    123\n    >>> max_element([0])\n    0\n    >>> max_element([-5, -1, -10])\n    -1\n    \"\"\"\n    if not l:\n        raise ValueError(\"max_element() arg is an empty sequence\")\n\n    max_val = l[0]\n    for element in l[1:]:\n        if element > max_val:\n            max_val = element\n    return max_val\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/36", "solution": "```python\ndef fizz_buzz(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Return the number of times the digit 7 appears in integers less than n which are divisible by 11 or 13.\n    >>> fizz_buzz(50)\n    0\n    >>> fizz_buzz(78)\n    2\n    >>> fizz_buzz(79)\n    3\n    \"\"\"\n    count_of_seven = 0\n    for i in range(1, n):\n        if (i % 11 == 0) or (i % 13 == 0):\n            # Convert the number to a string to easily check for the digit '7'\n            s_i = str(i)\n            for char_digit in s_i:\n                if char_digit == '7':\n                    count_of_seven += 1\n    return count_of_seven\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/37", "solution": "```python\ndef sort_even(l: list):\n    \"\"\"This function takes a list l and returns a list l' such that\n    l' is identical to l in the odd indicies, while its values at the even indicies are equal\n    to the values of the even indicies of l, but sorted.\n    >>> sort_even([1, 2, 3])\n    [1, 2, 3]\n    >>> sort_even([5, 6, 3, 4])\n    [3, 6, 5, 4]\n    \"\"\"\n    if not l:\n        return []\n\n    # Create a new list to store the result\n    result = [0] * len(l)\n\n    # Extract elements at even indices\n    even_indexed_values = []\n    for i in range(len(l)):\n        if i % 2 == 0:\n            even_indexed_values.append(l[i])\n        else:\n            # Copy odd-indexed elements directly to the result list\n            result[i] = l[i]\n\n    # Sort the extracted even-indexed values\n    even_indexed_values.sort()\n\n    # Place the sorted even-indexed values back into the result list at their original even positions\n    even_ptr = 0\n    for i in range(len(l)):\n        if i % 2 == 0:\n            result[i] = even_indexed_values[even_ptr]\n            even_ptr += 1\n\n    return result\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/38", "solution": "```python\ndef decode_cyclic(s: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"\n    Decodes a string that has been encoded by the encode_cyclic function.\n\n    The encoding process involves splitting the string into groups of three characters,\n    and for each group of three, cyclically shifting its characters such that the\n    first character moves to the second position, the second to the third, and the\n    third to the first (e.g., \"abc\" becomes \"bca\"). Groups with fewer than three\n    characters are left unchanged.\n\n    This function reverses that process.\n\n    Args:\n        s: A string that has been encoded.\n\n    Returns:\n        A string, the decoded version of the input string.\n\n    Examples:\n        >>> decode_cyclic(\"bcaefd\")\n        \"abcdef\"\n        >>> decode_cyclic(\"bcefa\")\n        \"abcfa\"\n        >>> decode_cyclic(\"\")\n        \"\"\n        >>> decode_cyclic(\"bca\")\n        \"abc\"\n        >>> decode_cyclic(\"efg\")\n        \"gef\"\n        >>> decode_cyclic(\"xyzw\")\n        \"wyzx\"\n        >>> decode_cyclic(\"a\")\n        \"a\"\n        >>> decode_cyclic(\"ab\")\n        \"ab\"\n    \"\"\"\n    if not s:\n        return \"\"\n\n    decoded_parts = []\n    n = len(s)\n    \n    # Iterate through the string in groups of three characters\n    for i in range(0, n, 3):\n        group = s[i:i+3]\n\n        # Apply the reverse cyclic shift only to groups of three\n        if len(group) == 3:\n            # The encoding was: original[0] -> group[1], original[1] -> group[2], original[2] -> group[0]\n            # To reverse:\n            # The original first character is group[2]\n            # The original second character is group[0]\n            # The original third character is group[1]\n            decoded_group = group[2] + group[0] + group[1]\n            decoded_parts.append(decoded_group)\n        else:\n            # Groups with fewer than three characters were left unchanged during encoding,\n            # so they remain unchanged during decoding.\n            decoded_parts.append(group)\n\n    # Join all decoded parts to form the final decoded string\n    return \"\".join(decoded_parts)\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/39", "solution": "import math\nfrom typing import Iterator\n\ndef _is_prime(num: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if a number is prime.\n\n    Args:\n        num: The integer to check for primality.\n\n    Returns:\n        True if the number is prime, False otherwise.\n    \"\"\"\n    if num <= 1:\n        return False\n    if num == 2:\n        return True\n    if num % 2 == 0:\n        return False\n    # Check for odd divisors from 3 up to sqrt(num)\n    for i in range(3, int(math.sqrt(num)) + 1, 2):\n        if num % i == 0:\n            return False\n    return True\n\ndef _fibonacci_generator() -> Iterator[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    An infinite generator for Fibonacci numbers.\n    Yields: 0, 1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, ...\n    \"\"\"\n    a, b = 0, 1\n    yield a\n    yield b\n    while True:\n        a, b = b, a + b\n        yield b\n\ndef prime_fib(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Returns the n-th number which is a Fibonacci number and also a prime number.\n\n    Args:\n        n: A positive integer representing the desired rank.\n\n    Returns:\n        The n-th prime Fibonacci number.\n\n    Raises:\n        ValueError: If n is not a positive integer.\n\n    Examples:\n        prime_fib(1) == 2\n        prime_fib(2) == 3\n        prime_fib(3) == 5\n        prime_fib(4) == 13\n        prime_fib(5) == 89\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(n, int) or n < 1:\n        raise ValueError(\"n must be a positive integer.\")\n\n    prime_fib_count = 0\n    fib_gen = _fibonacci_generator()\n\n    while True:\n        current_fib = next(fib_gen)\n\n        # Skip 0 and 1 as they are not prime by definition\n        # (Fibonacci sequence yields 0, 1, 1, 2, 3, 5, ...)\n        if current_fib <= 1:\n            continue\n\n        if _is_prime(current_fib):\n            prime_fib_count += 1\n            if prime_fib_count == n:\n                return current_fib"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/40", "solution": "```python\ndef triples_sum_to_zero(l: list):\n    \"\"\"\n    triples_sum_to_zero takes a list of integers as an input.\n    it returns True if there are three distinct elements in the list that\n    sum to zero, and False otherwise.\n\n    >>> triples_sum_to_zero([1, 3, 5, 0])\n    False\n    >>> triples_sum_to_zero([1, 3, -2, 1])\n    True\n    >>> triples_sum_to_zero([1, 2, 3, 7])\n    False\n    >>> triples_sum_to_zero([2, 4, -5, 3, 9, 7])\n    True\n    >>> triples_sum_to_zero([1])\n    False\n    >>> triples_sum_to_zero([])\n    False\n    >>> triples_sum_to_zero([-1, 0, 1, 2, -1, -4])\n    True\n    >>> triples_sum_to_zero([-5, -3, -1, 0, 1, 3, 5])\n    True\n    >>> triples_sum_to_zero([-2, -1, 0, 1, 2, 3])\n    True\n    >>> triples_sum_to_zero([-10, -5, 0, 5, 10])\n    True\n    >>> triples_sum_to_zero([0,0,0])\n    True\n    >>> triples_sum_to_zero([1,1,1])\n    False\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(l)\n    \n    # If there are less than 3 elements, it's impossible to form a triplet.\n    if n < 3:\n        return False\n\n    # Sort the list. This allows us to use the two-pointer technique efficiently.\n    # Sorting takes O(n log n) time.\n    sorted_l = sorted(l)\n\n    # Iterate through the list. For each element sorted_l[i], we try to find\n    # two other elements in the remaining part of the list that sum to -sorted_l[i].\n    # The outer loop runs up to n-2 times.\n    for i in range(n - 2):\n        # We need to find two elements in sorted_l[i+1...n-1] that sum to 'target'.\n        target = -sorted_l[i]\n        \n        # Initialize two pointers: 'left' starts just after 'i',\n        # and 'right' starts at the end of the list.\n        left = i + 1\n        right = n - 1\n\n        # The two-pointer loop runs at most (n-i-1) times for each 'i'.\n        # This makes the inner part O(n), leading to a total of O(n^2) after sorting.\n        while left < right:\n            current_sum = sorted_l[left] + sorted_l[right]\n\n            if current_sum == target:\n                # We found three distinct elements (at distinct indices i, left, right)\n                # that sum to zero.\n                return True\n            elif current_sum < target:\n                # The sum is too small, move 'left' pointer to a larger value.\n                left += 1\n            else:  # current_sum > target\n                # The sum is too large, move 'right' pointer to a smaller value.\n                right -= 1\n                \n    # If no such triplet is found after checking all possibilities, return False.\n    return False\n```"}
