{"task_id": "HumanEval/43", "solution": "```python\ndef pairs_sum_to_zero(l: list[int]) -> bool:\\\n    \"\"\"\n    pairs_sum_to_zero takes a list of integers as an input.\n    it returns True if there are two distinct elements in the list that\n    sum to zero, and False otherwise.\n    >>> pairs_sum_to_zero([1, 3, 5, 0])\n    False\n    >>> pairs_sum_to_zero([1, 3, -2, 1])\n    False\n    >>> pairs_sum_to_zero([1, 2, 3, 7])\n    False\n    >>> pairs_sum_to_zero([2, 4, -5, 3, 5, 7])\n    True\n    >>> pairs_sum_to_zero([1])\n    False\n    >>> pairs_sum_to_zero([])\n    False\n    >>> pairs_sum_to_zero([0, 0])\n    True\n    >>> pairs_sum_to_zero([5, -5, 5])\n    True\n    >>> pairs_sum_to_zero([0, 1, 2])\n    False\n    >>> pairs_sum_to_zero([-1, 1])\n    True\n    \"\"\"\n    seen = set()\n    for num in l:\\\n        # For each number 'num' in the list, we look for its additive inverse (complement).\n        # The complement is the number that, when added to 'num', results in zero.\n        # So, complement = 0 - num = -num.\n        complement = -num\n\n        # If this 'complement' is already in our 'seen' set, it means we have\n        # previously encountered an element in the list that, when combined\n        # with the current 'num', sums to zero.\n        # Since 'num' is the current element being processed and 'complement' \n        # was an element seen earlier, they are necessarily distinct elements\n        # (at distinct positions in the original list). This satisfies the\n        # \"two distinct elements\" requirement.\n        if complement in seen:\n            return True\n        \n        # If the complement is not found, add the current number to the set\n        # so it can be considered as a complement for subsequent numbers.\n        seen.add(num)\n    \n    # If the loop completes without finding any such pair, no two distinct\n    # elements sum to zero, so we return False.\n    return False\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/44", "solution": "```python\ndef change_base(x: int, base: int) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Change numerical base of input number x to base.\n    return string representation after the conversion.\n    base numbers are less than 10.\n    >>> change_base(8, 3)\n    '22'\n    >>> change_base(8, 2)\n    '1000'\n    >>> change_base(7, 2)\n    '111'\n    \"\"\"\n    if x == 0:\n        return '0'\n\n    if not (2 <= base < 10):\n        raise ValueError(\"Base must be between 2 and 9 (inclusive).\")\n\n    result = []\n    current_x = x\n    while current_x > 0:\n        remainder = current_x % base\n        result.append(str(remainder))\n        current_x //= base\n\n    # The remainders are collected in reverse order, so we need to reverse the list.\n    return \"\".join(result[::-1])\n```"}
